WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.540
It looked like the longest war on this side
of the planet had finally ended.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:10.170
For 52 years the Colombian government has
been fighting a civil war against the FARC,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:16.080
the revolutionary armed forces of Colombia,
a marxist guerilla organization.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.860
That have been bloody, and horrible, and have
cost over 220,000 lives and displaced over

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:22.860
5 million people.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.930
This has been going on since 1964.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:30.720
And peace negotiations have been in progress
for four years in Havana, Cuba.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.669
Finally, in June, there was a breakthrough.

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:38.050
Signed a ceasefire agreement today, for more
on this I'm joined by...

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.489
In September, President Santos of Colombia
and Timochenko, the leader of the FARC both

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:44.489
sides signed the deal.

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:46.489
It was a historic peace agreement.

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:52.640
Leaders, heads of state all over the world
came together, it was a massive celebration.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.870
All that was left was to put it to a vote,
to the people.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.030
It was a formality, part of the deal.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.039
The polls showed it winning.

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:02.539
The deal was going to pass.

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:03.649
And it didn’t.

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:04.800
Everyone was wrong.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.720
The polls were wrong.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.440
It was a really close vote - 50.2% voted against
it.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.690
Why would Colombians vote against such a momentous
peace agreement?

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.110
There are a few reasons.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.300
To understand, let’s go back to 1964.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:25.930
In the mid 60s, there were a lot of leftist,
marxist, communist type rebellions, particularly

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.030
in South America, and Latin America.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.890
You might recognize this guy.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.890
Or this one.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.430
They took over nearby Cuba.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:38.910
The FARC claimed to represent the interests
of the rural people in Colombia.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.340
There was intense inequality.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.200
Here's an example: 0.2 percent of farms owned
30% of farmland.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.410
This inequality had fueled a previous civil
war from 1946-1958, too, with 2 million peasant

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.460
farmers forced off their land.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.560
Manuel “Sureshot” Marulanda took up their
cause, forming FARC in the 60s after a government

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.380
assault on communist groups.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.370
He wanted the government to redistribute land
back to the poor, and rid the country of multinational

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:06.370
companies.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.549
But they ended up funding their fight in horrifying
ways.

00:02:09.549 --> 00:02:16.760
The FARC specifically got especially powerful
and wealthy from capitalizing on the drug

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.760
trade.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.129
Taking a lot of the wealth from the drug trade
and using that to kind of funnel back into

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:24.129
the rebellion.

00:02:24.129 --> 00:02:26.050
They used kidnappings and ransoms this way
too.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.269
Some of the kidnapped were forced to fight.

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:31.129
They recruited child soldiers.

00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:33.690
There was a lot of sexual violence, a lot
of rape.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.770
All this should give you a sense of why many
Colombians opposed the specifics of the peace

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.770
deal.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:44.330
The FARC agreed to end their drug trafficking,
to give away their weapons.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:52.650
They’d do community service removing their
landmines.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:03:00.290
They'd have turn over all of their weapons
to special UN inspectors who would be on the

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:01.290
ground.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.819
But in return, they’d face no jail time.

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:08.400
The Farc would also get 5 guaranteed seats
in each of Colombia’s two houses of congress,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.480
and financial aid for fighters who demobilize.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:17.459
The groups on the right and the government
also engaged in plenty of crimes, so it wasn't

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:18.790
just the FARC.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.939
This sounded like justice and forgiveness
to some.

00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:23.900
But to others it sounded like surrender.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:28.950
The previous president of Colombia, Uribe,
led the campaign to vote against the deal.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.650
He said repeatedly that he wasn’t against
peace, he wasn’t against a peace agreement

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.810
in principle.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.760
In fact he had led a crackdown on the FARC
that had brought them to the negotiating table

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.109
in the first place
– with heavy support from the US government.

00:03:42.109 --> 00:03:49.499
Top leadership was killed, a lot of people
demobilized, disarmed they went from 20,000

00:03:49.499 --> 00:03:53.230
fighters at their peak down to 6 or 7,000
fighters currently.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.450
It was effective, it kind of brought them
to the negotiating table.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.280
Uribe clearly wasn’t satisfied by their
outcome.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.419
This tweet is representative, “With these
agreements, there is no justice, nor truth.”

00:04:05.419 --> 00:04:10.159
But the thing is, if you look at a map of
how people voted, some of the areas that were

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:11.170
hit hardest by the conflict?

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:14.180
They voted yes most strongly.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:19.959
In Bojaya, the site of one of the FARC’s
worst massacres, bombing a church and killing

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:24.990
119 people, 96% voted for the peace agreement.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.870
And now everything is in complete disarray.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:29.190
It’s unclear what's even going to happen.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:34.180
But at this point, they cannot implement the
peace agreement as agreed.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:38.870
At least one thing is sure: the FARC and Colombia
have not gone back to war.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:50.479
At least not yet.

00:04:50.479 --> 00:05:09.230
And Santos, the current president, and Uribe,
the former, met for the first time in 6 years

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.729
and vowed to work toward peace.

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:16.460
Given anyone under 60 in Colombia has basically
only known a country at war, let’s hope

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:35.470
they succeed.

